&gt;&gt; 75?

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:39.842
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's great.

00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:43.034
&gt;&gt; Pretty good!
So we have so much to talk about today,

00:00:43.034 --> 00:00:47.849
I would love to start with Iran but
then eventually move on to IAC,

00:00:47.849 --> 00:00:50.550
Allen and co, Expedia, Uber.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.230
&gt;&gt; Sounds great.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:54.697
&gt;&gt; Great, so starting from the beginning,
your family,

00:00:54.697 --> 00:00:58.773
Seth was 7,500 miles aways from
here in the Bay Area in Iran.

00:00:58.773 --> 00:01:02.320
And that's where you grew up running
a hugely successful conglomerate.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.070
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:03.960
&gt;&gt; In 1978,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.580
you ended up having to flee, eventually
seeking asylum here in the United States.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.350
&gt;&gt; When you got here,
you didn't speak fluent English?

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.785
Sometimes you introduced yourself as
Daryn Kay instead of Dara Khosrowshahi.

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:18.388
&gt;&gt; Those were dark days, yes.

00:01:18.388 --> 00:01:22.592
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How did the challenge of

00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:25.860
assimilation shape you as a leader?

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.490
&gt;&gt; So I think, first of all,
I was a kid, I was nine years old.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:35.570
And I think that children, as you know,
are much more flexible and

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:40.530
they can assimilate much more easily into
any situation, different situations.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.370
But I was also the youngest brother, and

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.630
I was one of the younger
cousins in the family.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.570
So I was very,
very low on the totem pole and

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:53.179
I had to assimilate even
before I came to the states.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:55.063
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Whatever was going on,

00:01:55.063 --> 00:01:57.110
I had no say in the matter.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:02.190
And coming to the states for us,
what we had was, we always had family.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:07.440
And that was a constant and
is a constant for

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.390
me in that really I certainly was thrown

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.870
into a new environment but I always had
the protection of family to come home to.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.710
We were very lucky to have a very
strong education in Iran, and

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:24.280
we continue to have a strong
education in the US.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.041
I learned a lesson that if you don't
understand what someone is saying,

00:02:30.041 --> 00:02:34.660
the answer is no, not yes,
because it's an Iranian thing,

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.610
you always want to say, yes.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:39.192
So I'd have no idea what someone was
saying, I'd be like, yes, yes, and then

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:42.678
I'd be like playing football someplace,
having no idea how to catch a ball.

00:02:42.678 --> 00:02:44.425
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But for

00:02:44.425 --> 00:02:48.700
me the assimilation that really
got us in was through sports.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:56.618
Soccer was kind of the game of
the world and not so popular in the US,

00:02:56.618 --> 00:03:00.939
so we were regarded at soccer
gods at my high school.

00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:03.544
And that's how we got to
know a lot of other kids and

00:03:03.544 --> 00:03:06.150
really assimilate it into the school.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.603
&gt;&gt; Do you keep up with sports?

00:03:07.603 --> 00:03:11.475
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, of course,
I'm a big Premiership soccer fan,

00:03:11.475 --> 00:03:16.307
I wake up at 5:30 in the morning, my wife
says I'm crazy but she's good with hit.

00:03:16.307 --> 00:03:19.540
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So
it's hard to know your personal identity,

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.710
you came here as a child and
you found your way.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.295
It's also hard to know your
professional identity,

00:03:25.295 --> 00:03:31.270
when you were at Allen and
Company, you met Barry Diller.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.401
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes.
&gt;&gt; And he has a reputation where

00:03:33.401 --> 00:03:36.643
some comment that he is demanding but
loyal,

00:03:36.643 --> 00:03:40.500
you identified him as someone
you wanted as a mentor.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:45.792
How did you know that, and what advice can
you give us on cultivating mentorship?

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.413
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:55.268
He was my first true mentor, and
the way that I met him at Allen

00:03:55.268 --> 00:04:00.310
&amp; Company,
I was a young analyst working on a deal.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:05.560
This was a hostile tender offer that Barry
had launched for Paramount Pictures.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:10.123
He was running QVC,
there was a deal for Viacom,

00:04:10.123 --> 00:04:15.928
which was is a big conglomerate,
to buy Paramount Pictures.

00:04:15.928 --> 00:04:21.400
And Barry was really interested in the
combination of commerce and entertainment.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:27.140
And when Paramount came in to play,
he went after it, and

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:33.820
I was an analyst on the deal, and
he's like this giant Hollywood mogul.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.803
And this was a really dramatic
offer where we were making bids and

00:04:37.803 --> 00:04:39.736
then they'd make bids, etc.

00:04:39.736 --> 00:04:43.394
It was a very competitive
public battle for

00:04:43.394 --> 00:04:47.930
control of this highly
strategic asset at the time.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:52.055
And the thing that I remember about
Barry is, somehow he found out.

00:04:52.055 --> 00:04:56.473
And he would deal with an SVP who
had a VP and an associate, and

00:04:56.473 --> 00:05:01.300
somehow he found out that I
was running the deal model.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.914
And I was on the trading floor,
just this nobody,

00:05:03.914 --> 00:05:06.350
he found out that I was
running the deal model.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:10.483
And he comes up to me, he's like, you're
the one running the deal model, right?

00:05:10.483 --> 00:05:13.517
Yes, Mr. Diller, and he's like, okay,

00:05:13.517 --> 00:05:18.120
I want you to explain to me exactly
how it works, and I said, when?

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.850
He said, right now, and of course
when Barry Diller says, right now,

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.670
you say, okay.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:28.130
So I sat down with him and he wanted
to know everything about the deal model

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.790
from the person who had actually built it.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:35.610
And he took the time and
I learned as I got to know Barry,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:40.611
that one of the things that he really
believes in is going to the source.

00:05:40.611 --> 00:05:43.859
That every time you get
information that is filtered,

00:05:43.859 --> 00:05:46.200
you lose fidelity in the information.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:53.600
This was a really important deal for him,
and he, instead of being comfortable

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.192
with someone who would present information
to him in a clean and comfortable way.

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:02.027
He wanted to be uncomfortable
with an unknown but

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:07.090
knowing that he'd be closer to the truth,
and that was how our relationship started.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.470
And he would come to me on the deal model,
are we bidding to much,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.660
are we bidding to little?

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:16.442
He was making the decisions but
he really wanted to go to the source and

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:19.502
understand the core of the subject matter.

00:06:19.502 --> 00:06:24.979
We lost that battle, and
I still remember he released

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:29.980
a statement saying,
we lost, they won, next.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:35.540
When I read that thing, I'm like,
I want to be a part of next.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:43.290
So with that, we went after a bunch
of other deals and machinations.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:47.710
And when the predecessor
company to IAC was born,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:49.710
it was Home Shopping Network
at the the time.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.730
He sold QVC,
took control of Home Shopping Network.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:58.850
We closed on a very large deal to
buy an entertainment business,

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.900
Universal Television.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:07.750
And at that point he said, well, I want
you to work exclusively as my deal person.

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:12.830
I said, I can't do that,
I've got a portfolio, he said, I want your

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:18.200
portfolio to be me, and I said, well then
give me a job, and he said, let's go.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.903
So that's when I jumped
on board with him and

00:07:20.903 --> 00:07:24.202
the one lesson that I learned
from Allen and Company.

00:07:24.202 --> 00:07:26.977
Herbert Allen was the CEO at the time,

00:07:26.977 --> 00:07:31.746
he always said that as an investor,
he didn't bet on companies,

00:07:31.746 --> 00:07:37.640
he bet on people, he just said,
bet on people, I make my bet on people.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.120
And I think one lesson for

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.200
me that I took away was, I was
an investor, I was an employee, right?

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.730
But instead of making a bet on the job or

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:51.980
instead of making a bet on the company,
I was making a bet on a person.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:55.120
And I said, Barry Diller, this is
the person that I want to work with,

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:57.940
whatever you want me to do, I'll do it.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:00.720
And I followed him and
it's been a great decision of my life.

00:08:02.036 --> 00:08:04.050
He eventually sponsored you.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.210
&gt;&gt; Yes, very much so.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:08.436
&gt;&gt; And
tapped you to be the CEO of Expedia.

00:08:08.436 --> 00:08:08.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.780
&gt;&gt; And this was 14 years out of college.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:16.030
&gt;&gt; I guess so, yeah,
must have been desperate.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.990
&gt;&gt; Well, you proved yourself, right?

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:18.690
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:25.504
At Expedia, you grew the business
from 2 to $10 billion over 13 years,

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:27.830
that's very impressive.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:31.049
At the same time your competitor,
Priceline,

00:08:31.049 --> 00:08:35.482
they were a distant competitor at
the time you started being CEO.

00:08:35.482 --> 00:08:39.137
And then eventually actually became the
leader in the online travel agency space.

00:08:39.137 --> 00:08:42.974
&gt;&gt; Yes, it is very painful
even to hear you say it, yes.

00:08:42.974 --> 00:08:46.319
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It grew to four times the size of

00:08:46.319 --> 00:08:48.259
Expedia when you left.

00:08:48.259 --> 00:08:53.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Twist it in!

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:56.300
&gt;&gt; You had to so
many successes, I am curious

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.290
what you think your key wins were and
what you would have done differently.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:06.810
&gt;&gt; I think I'll talk about
booking.com since, or

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.990
price, well is that booking
group since you brought it up.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:15.897
This was a company that we looked at,
and Expedia was one of the big players

00:09:15.897 --> 00:09:20.040
in the travel industry that was
growing incredibly quickly.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:26.340
And we were taking on the offline travel
agents and the traditional players.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:32.200
And what we saw with Booking.com was
a player who had very, very low margins.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:39.000
And what prevented us from ultimately
competing with them the way that we should

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:46.130
have was that we didn't want to give
up our own fat markets, to be frank.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:52.060
And it goes to the whole issue
of the innovator's dilemma.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:55.520
We saw this business, and listen, we had
an opportunity to buy them just like

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.850
Priceline actually bought booking.com.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:02.479
And booking.com became 95% of the business
and if we named the business booking.com,

00:10:02.479 --> 00:10:05.990
we'd have the same opportunities
about our company.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:11.159
But in looking at that company, our
team poo pooed it because it was a less,

00:10:11.159 --> 00:10:14.946
the unit economics of that
model didn't look as good.

00:10:14.946 --> 00:10:17.668
And instead of looking
at that as an asset,

00:10:17.668 --> 00:10:20.020
our team looked at it as a liability.

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:27.410
And for me, that's a very important
lesson in that it's much easier for

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:31.930
a business to make all
the tough decisions.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:37.660
And shape their business based on low
economics and low margins and then expand.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:42.145
And expansion is inevitably faster if you
have a low price product, especially in

00:10:42.145 --> 00:10:46.870
the Internet world where price is
something that is easily discoverable.

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:52.700
It's much easier to go from low margin and
expand than be a high margin player and

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.250
then slowly bring your margin down.

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.550
We did bring our margin down over a long
period of time but if I had to do

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:04.670
something quicker, I would've taken the
pain up front, I would've done it faster.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:08.915
And I think that for us, as a company,
when we started taken that margin pain.

00:11:08.915 --> 00:11:13.471
And when we started really rethinking
our business not as a travel company but

00:11:13.471 --> 00:11:15.410
as a technology company.

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:20.700
That was the term that Expedia had to
go through to create value for us.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:25.010
But it's absolutely a lesson learned,
which is, low margins are an asset,

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:26.090
they're not a liability.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:31.290
And to lose that cost discipline

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:35.640
ultimately is going to cost you
market share over the long term.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:41.744
&gt;&gt; When did you realize that, and
how quickly did you adapt at Expedia?

00:11:41.744 --> 00:11:46.940
&gt;&gt; We realized it actually pretty quickly,
and the issue was that we had to

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:52.583
go from a situation where a transaction
was worth, call it 25% or 15%.

00:11:52.583 --> 00:11:58.550
So we had to essentially engineer 40%
of our revenues out of the model.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:05.460
And that was a process, it took us
a couple years to realize it and

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:10.180
then we started engineering it over
a period of two or three years.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.750
What allowed us to engineer,

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:17.280
it was really making the bet on
our core technology platforms.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:22.000
And for me, one of the toughest things
that I went through at Expedia was that

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:23.300
Expedia was a holding company,

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.030
a number of different brands
within the holding company.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:30.750
And the largest part of the business
was Expedia.com, flagship,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.550
full service online travel agency.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:39.910
And I had failed twice in finding
a leader for that business.

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:43.536
Found a person, it didn't work out,
brought in another person,

00:12:43.536 --> 00:12:45.280
it didn't work out.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.783
And this was a particularly tough time for
me in the board, and

00:12:47.783 --> 00:12:48.997
I went to the board and Barry.

00:12:48.997 --> 00:12:52.785
I said, I've gotta bring in
another person, if I'm 0 for 3,

00:12:52.785 --> 00:12:57.170
I think you should fire me,
Barry said, you're damn right.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:01.790
So I said, then my third pick is going to
be me, I don't understand what it takes to

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:06.840
run this business, and the only way I'll
understand is if I run it for awhile.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:10.180
So I was the CEO,
the corporate parent, and

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:15.520
then I was the CEO,
the operational CEO of Expedia.com.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.590
And I learned more during those four,

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:22.200
five years when I ran Expedia.com
than I ever learned in my life.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.160
And I learned what it really
means to operate a company.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.900
I learned what it really means for
business and

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:31.770
technology to come together,
to really take risks,

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:37.600
to really accelerate the rate of
innovation within a company operationally.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:42.270
And once we started turning
Expedia.com around,

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:44.310
the company overall
starting turning around.

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:47.970
And I was lucky enough then
to hand that business off to

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:51.550
the CTO of Expedia.com at the time,
who's still running that business.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:55.493
So that was the real big turn, and for
me, it was a great learning opportunity.

00:13:55.493 --> 00:14:00.401
And I know I would not be in this spot had
I not made the mistake of not being able

00:14:00.401 --> 00:14:03.722
to find the right person
through on Expedia.com.

00:14:05.503 --> 00:14:09.659
&gt;&gt; Moving on from Expedia, which-
&gt;&gt; From that painful loss to booking,

00:14:09.659 --> 00:14:10.420
thank you.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:12.315
&gt;&gt; Well, it's a win in the end, right?

00:14:12.315 --> 00:14:13.260
2 to 10.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:13.760
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:21.300
&gt;&gt; In August, 2017, not too long ago, the
Uber board selected you to be their CEO.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:22.200
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:26.230
&gt;&gt; And I would presume that each of you,
the board and

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:29.810
yourself, had requests of each other.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:30.814
Reportedly, Barry Diller,

00:14:30.814 --> 00:14:32.829
helped you during this
three-week negotiation process.

00:14:32.829 --> 00:14:34.709
&gt;&gt; Very much so, yeah.

00:14:34.709 --> 00:14:37.990
&gt;&gt; It wasn't a negotiation process,
it was an interview process, really.

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:45.610
&gt;&gt; Okay, Either way, we can talk about
the negotiation or the interview.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:49.620
But I'm most curious about
the negotiation and if you can offer us

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:53.475
any advice on negotiating job offers,
although on a much smaller scale?

00:14:53.475 --> 00:14:56.995
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, let me tell you.

00:14:56.995 --> 00:15:01.530
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So one of the advantages that I had in

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:09.548
approaching this role was that
I had a great job already.

00:15:09.548 --> 00:15:15.300
And I loved what I was doing at Expedia,
and I really wasn't looking to jump ship.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:20.230
And it was both part of
the interview process and

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:23.650
the negotiation process where,
during the interview process.

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:28.510
I was just dead honest about
what I brought to the table and

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:32.770
what I thought I needed in order
to succeed at the company.

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:37.640
And I still remember,
there was the various candidates,

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:40.900
and there were other candidates
who were much better known.

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.840
They were capital letters candidates,
they came in and presented to the board.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:51.020
And I made a presentation
to the board where I

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:55.750
talked about my expectations of the board.

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:59.730
And part of the expectations for the board
were, what happens in the boardroom stays

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:02.910
in the boardroom,
doesn't get tweeted out someplace, right?

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:07.510
And also that with
the founders of the company

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:12.231
being very much a part of
the company spirit and

00:16:12.231 --> 00:16:16.119
culture and what built that company.

00:16:16.119 --> 00:16:19.591
If they really wanted a new
leader of that company,

00:16:19.591 --> 00:16:22.839
it needed to be very clear
who their leader was.

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:28.496
And that I expected the board
to be there for me.

00:16:28.496 --> 00:16:33.005
But I needed the freedom to
maneuver appropriately and

00:16:33.005 --> 00:16:37.219
make changes that I felt
needed to be made quickly,

00:16:37.219 --> 00:16:40.620
because this was a tough situation.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:42.904
So I was very straight with
the board about those issues.

00:16:42.904 --> 00:16:47.128
And I remember one of the board
members after the meeting said,

00:16:47.128 --> 00:16:51.670
all of it was great but that page
was really, it was not such a great,

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.561
it was not that well
received by the board.

00:16:54.561 --> 00:16:58.475
I said well, if it wasn't that
well received, then I'm glad,

00:16:58.475 --> 00:17:00.600
because this is important to me.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:04.160
And for whatever reason,
maybe they ignore that page.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:10.367
But I just think that when you get
into a situation in a new opportunity,

00:17:10.367 --> 00:17:14.196
or a new opportunity that presents itself,

00:17:14.196 --> 00:17:18.560
being transparent about
your own capabilities.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:22.754
And I think you've gotta go first.

00:17:22.754 --> 00:17:26.237
And hopefully then getting
the transparency back from the other party

00:17:26.237 --> 00:17:28.190
that you're talking about.

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:29.570
Ultimately, it's a win-win,

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:35.374
because you never want to get yourself in
a position where you're at the wrong job,

00:17:35.374 --> 00:17:41.080
you don't have the capabilities, etc, and
vice versa, it's a loss for both party.

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:45.604
So I've taken this, one thing that
I've learned from Barry is he's

00:17:45.604 --> 00:17:50.215
just the most transparent person on earth,
he just lays it out there.

00:17:50.215 --> 00:17:53.277
I think Diane von Furstenberg was here,
and

00:17:53.277 --> 00:17:57.263
I agree she was pretty transparent
about a lot of things.

00:17:57.263 --> 00:17:59.256
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Just,

00:17:59.256 --> 00:18:03.273
it's part of that core
culture that he taught me.

00:18:03.273 --> 00:18:05.333
It's something that I took to that board,

00:18:05.333 --> 00:18:08.608
we have some very frank discussions
about expectations both ways.

00:18:08.608 --> 00:18:11.780
And it's something that since
I've taken to the company.

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:14.780
I think in the second town
hall that we had as company,

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:19.250
I actually took my board presentation,
and I presented that at the all hands.

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:22.832
I said, this is me, this is actually
what I talked to the board about.

00:18:22.832 --> 00:18:29.360
And it's the same deck, and
this is what you're going to get with me.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.450
And then we go on from there.

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:34.870
&gt;&gt; As you said, it was a tough decision-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, very much so.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:40.279
&gt;&gt; To move to Uber from Expedia,
you had a clear idea of what you wanted.

00:18:40.279 --> 00:18:43.568
And one of our most sought-after
classes here at the GSB,

00:18:43.568 --> 00:18:47.936
called Leadership Perspectives with
Joel Peterson and Charles O'Reilly,

00:18:47.936 --> 00:18:51.040
we often ask visiting leaders
what winning is to them.

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:56.144
You traded the top job at Expedia,
where you were very happy and

00:18:56.144 --> 00:18:59.490
just doing tremendous things,
for this job at Uber.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:02.410
Which some of the press described
as a lose-lose situation.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.571
&gt;&gt; I obviously wasn't reading that press,
but okay.

00:19:05.571 --> 00:19:09.694
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Ultimately, what is winning to you,

00:19:09.694 --> 00:19:11.970
how did you think about that?

00:19:14.743 --> 00:19:16.682
&gt;&gt; Honestly,
I wasn't thinking about winning.

00:19:18.146 --> 00:19:21.090
I'll relate one story,
which was a funny story.

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:26.760
When I was first reached out for
the job by some headhunters,

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:30.050
I said, no way, I was happy where I was.

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:34.360
And then I was having a drink
with a friend of mine,

00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:39.890
actually Daniel Ek,
the CEO of Spotify, at a conference.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:41.460
And he asked me, he said,
have they reached out to you?

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:44.930
Because I told them,
I think you'd be perfect for this role.

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:50.450
I'd say yeah, but I'm happy,
I'm doing great at Expedia.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:53.800
I'm not going to,
why would I ever do that?

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:58.028
And he looks at me and he says, Dara,
since when is life about happiness?

00:19:58.028 --> 00:20:01.018
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And he stared at me with his

00:20:01.018 --> 00:20:03.817
Scandinavian eyes, and-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:03.817 --> 00:20:04.583
&gt;&gt; And he said,

00:20:04.583 --> 00:20:07.360
it's about doing something great.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:11.910
And my wife was there, Syd was there
as well, and she's like, come on,

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.240
man, you should go for this.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:18.250
So between those two, that was kind of
the moment that that changed for me.

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:26.490
And listen, there are very few times in
your life when you can change the world.

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:29.220
Or you can change something
that's truly important.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:35.452
And for me, the advice that I always give
folks that I talk to as far as careers is,

00:20:35.452 --> 00:20:41.707
I think that people often optimize way too
much for the role or the company, etc.

00:20:41.707 --> 00:20:44.639
The first thing that I always optimized
for is, who are you going to work with?

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:49.528
And the people that I met on that board,
even though there were questions

00:20:49.528 --> 00:20:53.656
about the board, they were excellent,
the folks that I talked to on the board.

00:20:53.656 --> 00:20:55.522
And the second and third are,

00:20:55.522 --> 00:21:00.260
can you go to place where you
personally can make a difference?

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:03.780
And can you go to a place
that's making a difference?

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:07.350
I was in investment banking,
where I was making a difference, but

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:10.860
I didn't feel like the impact
on the world was quite there.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:14.960
And this Uber opportunity for
me was, this is an extraordinary

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:19.200
opportunity to make an impact at a company
that's making an impact in the world.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:23.359
And my attitude was,
it's not about winning or losing,

00:21:23.359 --> 00:21:26.057
it's actually about taking a shot.

00:21:26.057 --> 00:21:33.650
And if I took a shot and I won, then
glory come upon me, or whatever it is.

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:36.040
But even if I took a shot and
I failed, and

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:39.000
I don't plan to fail,
I took that fricking shot.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:43.710
And when I'm 30 years from now, 40 years
from now, I'm going to have a great,

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:46.620
great story to tell my kids and
their kids and their kids.

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:50.800
That, to me, is the definition of winning,
winning is getting in the game,

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:53.450
is playing the game.

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:56.668
And for me, a big part of
winning is winning as a team,

00:21:56.668 --> 00:21:58.900
I don't want to win alone.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:03.098
It's so much more fun to
celebrate with teammates, it's so

00:22:03.098 --> 00:22:06.652
much more fun to celebrate as a tribe,
as a family.

00:22:06.652 --> 00:22:12.915
So for me winning is, I'm playing in
a game and it's a really important game.

00:22:12.915 --> 00:22:16.110
And I've got a team around me, and
if we win, it's going to be a great story.

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:20.830
&gt;&gt; One of the central tenants you
mentioned was going to a place where you

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:21.700
can have impact.

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:22.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:26.090
&gt;&gt; To be sure, there was a lot
to do at Uber when you came on.

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:30.870
And in particular, Uber has gone through
hell because of accusations around Me Too,

00:22:30.870 --> 00:22:33.180
unprofessional management, safety issues.

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:35.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, deserved, in some.

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:40.950
&gt;&gt; The culture was thought to
over-index on this culture of

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.090
begging forgiveness
versus asking permission.

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:47.930
How did you address that specifically,
and what's left to do?

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:53.517
&gt;&gt; So I think first of all, the,
I don't know if the company,

00:22:53.517 --> 00:22:57.131
well, I kind of know that the company
wouldn't have succeeded and

00:22:57.131 --> 00:23:01.280
been in that positon in the first
place without that culture.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:04.244
So I know that's it very to easy to
create a size it after the fact, etc.

00:23:04.244 --> 00:23:08.999
But the fact is that there are very few
companies that have created the kind of

00:23:08.999 --> 00:23:13.101
brand and the kind of service that
that team created in the world.

00:23:13.101 --> 00:23:16.140
It was historic, it is historic,
it will be historic.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:20.730
But the one thing that I've
learned is success imprints

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.410
much more forcefully than failure.

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:27.118
And what the pattern that you
see with companies is that,

00:23:27.118 --> 00:23:30.290
anytime you have a success,
of course it's because of you and

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:32.736
everything that you do and
your culture, etc.

00:23:32.736 --> 00:23:36.304
If there's a failure, it's the market,
or the market wasn't right,

00:23:36.304 --> 00:23:38.810
timing wasn't right,
it's always someone else.

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:43.650
So success sometimes, and
the kind of success that Uber had,

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:48.240
was so extraordinary, that actually
in hindsight it kind of made sense.

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:52.096
The success was born out of aggression,
and going for it,

00:23:52.096 --> 00:23:56.125
and not taking no for an answer,
and running through doors.

00:23:56.125 --> 00:24:01.507
And it worked, and
it worked spectacularly,

00:24:01.507 --> 00:24:05.490
but that hid Some negatives and

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:09.800
some very significant negatives that we're
forming as a result to that culture.

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:13.840
And I think that

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:17.230
I have an experience that kind of
success that Uber experience early on.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:20.992
I don't know if I would have seen it
because they're too busy winning.

00:24:20.992 --> 00:24:24.990
And they're too busy being on
the photo spread of a magazine

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:27.980
talking about how they're changing
the world and how they can do no wrong.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:35.550
So I want to be careful about how we think
about this situation, where we wound up

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:40.710
was that once the winning stopped,
the fall was really, really tough.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.290
And it happened suddenly.

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:46.985
And I think the company
hadn't built the bulwark and

00:24:46.985 --> 00:24:52.119
the framework to be able to
resist the wall that they hit.

00:24:52.119 --> 00:24:53.970
And they needed a change.

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:58.500
And I'm here and
I can be a part of that change.

00:24:58.500 --> 00:25:02.848
That said, what we wound up with
was a culture that was, I think,

00:25:02.848 --> 00:25:06.160
not diverse enough.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:07.280
There was one way, and

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:11.690
there was the only way that was
defined kind of as a Uber way.

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:16.647
The company had not taking the time
to think about diversity of thought,

00:25:16.647 --> 00:25:19.580
diversity of origin, gender diversity.

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:26.257
Thinking about under represented
minorities and their position

00:25:26.257 --> 00:25:31.117
at the company, I think that the company
was a little bit too top down and

00:25:31.117 --> 00:25:34.762
it's part of the power of
the founder like company but

00:25:34.762 --> 00:25:39.075
it can also be part of the weakness
of the founder like company.

00:25:39.075 --> 00:25:41.780
And I think these are all
actions that we're taking.

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:45.170
This is a company,
we are truly a global company.

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:48.290
The connection that we have with
the cities that we operate in,

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:49.610
we're not just the digital company.

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:55.130
The connection that we have with the
cities that we operate in are physical and

00:25:55.130 --> 00:25:55.940
are fundamental.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:26:01.260
When our payment system goes down,
we have drivers who don't get paid,

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:05.180
and some of them need to get paid in
order to afford dinner that night.

00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:06.870
So the connection that we have for

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:11.850
the cities with our driver partners,
with our riders, are so fundamental and

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:16.050
so important that it's incredibly
important as we develop as company,

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:20.720
to be as diverse as the business the
riders and the drivers that we're serving.

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:24.440
To be diverse in our views,
in our international viewpoints, etc.

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:28.900
And we are working very hard in bringing
a leadership team that values that

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:34.700
diversity, and then pushing down the
culture of truly celebrating differences.

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:39.070
Truly being at one and in service of
the cities in which we operate, but

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:42.700
it's a journey, it's only been an year,
and it's a start, but it's something that

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:46.470
I and I think our leadership team
are incredibly passionate about.

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:49.530
And the opportunity for us is,

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:53.790
I think there's this dislocation
between technology companies and

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:57.110
the rest of the world, and
it's coming out in a lot of ways.

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:58.250
We're not the only tech company.

00:26:58.250 --> 00:27:01.905
We're in some sense
the tip of the spear here.

00:27:01.905 --> 00:27:04.110
Bu I think as a technology company,

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:07.930
we touch people in a way that
other tech companies don't.

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:11.280
It's fundamental, I think we're touching
the cities in which we operate.

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:12.940
So I think we can,

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:17.900
we as Uber, have the opportunity to be
that next generation technology leader.

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:23.020
Both in terms of culture, but
actually how we connect to the cities and

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:27.320
the constituencies that we work with and
operate around.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:29.900
So it's a big job, but I'm psyched for it.

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:34.180
&gt;&gt; What's one specific example of a seed
that you're sowing right now that

00:27:34.180 --> 00:27:38.450
could actually, ultimately look like what
you're describing as an ideal stunt?

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:40.910
&gt;&gt; We're thinking about
our driver-partners.

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:43.040
We've always called our
driver-partners partners, but

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:47.350
I don't think we've treated
them as such in the past.

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:52.433
So now when we are launching our product,
the driver app,

00:27:52.433 --> 00:27:58.475
we actually go to our driver partners,
we do the research with them.

00:27:58.475 --> 00:28:02.520
For the first time, really,
we got driver partner beta testers.

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:06.370
And go out there, and we actually build
not what we think they should have, but

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:08.610
actually what they're telling
us they should have as well.

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:13.840
So it's just a very different way of
building product than we've had in

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:14.465
the past.

00:28:14.465 --> 00:28:16.962
One of the areas where
we're thinking about for

00:28:16.962 --> 00:28:18.904
our driver partners are insurance.

00:28:18.904 --> 00:28:24.640
It's what if they get in an accident,
maternity, paternity leave, etc.

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:29.430
We have partnered with AXA in Europe to

00:28:29.430 --> 00:28:33.120
actually provide these kinds
of insurance coverages for

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:36.860
our driver partners and it's something
that's been incredibly well received.

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:42.220
So that might not have
been a direction that old

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:46.770
Uber would've gone through, but
I think that a more mature Uber, an Uber

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:52.240
that understands that our driver partners,
in many ways are the face of the company.

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:54.780
That's an area that we invest in,
and I think more and more,

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:57.110
we're looking to treat them as
the true partners that they are.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:00.410
&gt;&gt; Look at the external side of the house.

00:29:00.410 --> 00:29:04.270
There's a lot that you've been doing
internally, and you mentioned that another

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:08.760
one of your tenants is going to a place
that actually can have an impact, and

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:13.280
indeed, Uber has shown and
demonstrated that potential.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:17.480
A former high-ranking Google executive
recently said to the New Yorker that,

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:19.100
quote, everyone is terrified of Uber.

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:22.610
Your comm-
&gt;&gt; Who is that person?

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:23.865
&gt;&gt; Anonymous New Yorker.

00:29:23.865 --> 00:29:27.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So your competition is gunning for you.

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:28.300
&gt;&gt; Yes, or course.

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:32.278
&gt;&gt; And one of our beloved lecturers here,
Rob Seigal, often asks and

00:29:32.278 --> 00:29:36.055
popularized the following
question to visiting executives.

00:29:36.055 --> 00:29:40.390
You have one silver bullet to
take out a competitor, who is it?

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:42.600
&gt;&gt; Wow-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:44.400
&gt;&gt; That is a tough one.

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:51.091
A silver bullet to take out a competitor.

00:29:51.091 --> 00:29:53.767
Might be a pink competitor, I'm not sure.

00:29:53.767 --> 00:29:58.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:01.260
I honestly think that right now,

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:06.580
I actually do very much believe that
companies are too competitor focused

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:14.780
sometimes and many times stop caring about
whats important, which is the customer.

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:16.830
If there's a silver bullet for us,

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:22.810
it's actually really starting, and I
think Uber, we have been, to some extent,

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:27.350
historically too guilty of competitors and
what they're doing and share, etc.

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:33.540
We are now really, our operations
teams and our product teams and

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:39.644
our technology teams are working together
in a way that is customer-obsessed.

00:30:39.644 --> 00:30:43.152
And our customers are the writers and
the drivers and that is,

00:30:43.152 --> 00:30:47.290
if there's a silver bullet, I don't
want to shoot it at the competitor.

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:51.750
I actually want us to accelerate
this kind of new customer obsession

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:55.490
as a company because ultimately,
whether we win or lose,

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:58.000
it ain't going to be about
what the competitors do.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:30:59.822
It's about what we do.

00:30:59.822 --> 00:31:02.410
We have a global scope.

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:04.250
None of our competitors
have a global scope.

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:05.540
We are multi-product.

00:31:05.540 --> 00:31:09.520
We've got a rides business that is
enormous and growing very quickly.

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:13.320
We have an incredibly
business growing with hits.

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:16.135
We are getting to scooters and bikes.

00:31:16.135 --> 00:31:18.450
We're getting into vitove,

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:23.020
vertical take off landing products,
we're getting into frame, etc.

00:31:23.020 --> 00:31:26.780
The definition of success we're
taking on so many competitors

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:30.940
that I need a silver shooting machine
gun to take out my competitors.

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:33.970
So it's really we've gotta be
focused internally in order to win.

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:36.170
That's the real focus for us.

00:31:36.170 --> 00:31:37.650
&gt;&gt; Lots of product lines and

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:40.320
on top of that you're planning
on going public next year.

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:41.371
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:31:41.371 --> 00:31:46.010
&gt;&gt; We talked about this a little bit,
and specifically in the context of Uber,

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:48.546
how do you trade off
investing in growth or

00:31:48.546 --> 00:31:53.856
in the new products with demonstrating
predictable profitability to shareholders?

00:31:53.856 --> 00:31:55.760
&gt;&gt; We're not going to have
predictable profitability.

00:31:57.580 --> 00:32:01.120
And we'll say it to our shareholders,
and the shareholders can choose.

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:04.735
If they want a predictably
profitable company, go buy a bank.

00:32:04.735 --> 00:32:07.889
Don't come to us, simple.

00:32:07.889 --> 00:32:10.352
&gt;&gt; I notice when you were
describing you're [LAUGH]

00:32:10.352 --> 00:32:10.882
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]

00:32:10.882 --> 00:32:13.531
&gt;&gt; I just, I'd rather be truthful.

00:32:13.531 --> 00:32:16.378
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I can't wait for

00:32:16.378 --> 00:32:17.439
your shareholder letter.

00:32:17.439 --> 00:32:22.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I did have, when I was at Expedia,

00:32:22.630 --> 00:32:27.886
I had a, I still remember an investor
meeting with an investor and

00:32:27.886 --> 00:32:32.967
he talked for like 5 minutes
about being a long term investor.

00:32:32.967 --> 00:32:35.924
We're a long term investor,
we're going to be an owner with you etc.

00:32:35.924 --> 00:32:40.916
I'm like so what's your average holding
period and he looked at me in like,

00:32:40.916 --> 00:32:43.881
with those deep eyes and
he said Nine months.

00:32:43.881 --> 00:32:46.342
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was like, my god.

00:32:46.342 --> 00:32:51.829
So it's just, you've got to,
there are true long term investors but

00:32:51.829 --> 00:32:55.642
just like you can't be
too competitor focused,

00:32:55.642 --> 00:32:59.281
just focus on the product,
state who you are.

00:32:59.281 --> 00:33:04.084
And that comes with limits and I think you
will get investors that you deserve and

00:33:04.084 --> 00:33:07.022
the markets that we're going after are so
big and

00:33:07.022 --> 00:33:09.259
the position that we have is unique.

00:33:09.259 --> 00:33:11.577
And by the way, we have lots of tough,

00:33:11.577 --> 00:33:16.432
tough competition that we will find
investors who hopefully grew with us that,

00:33:16.432 --> 00:33:19.342
that really the long
term is what we're after.

00:33:19.342 --> 00:33:24.119
And what I like to talk to,
to my team about is,

00:33:24.119 --> 00:33:28.293
I'm actually short-term inpatient.

00:33:28.293 --> 00:33:32.528
I want the small ideas that take
a week and I'm long term patient,

00:33:32.528 --> 00:33:37.471
I'll take the big ideas that take five
years, I don't like the in between.

00:33:37.471 --> 00:33:40.953
And so if there's a small idea,
get it to me,

00:33:40.953 --> 00:33:45.369
get it launched in a month,
get it launched in two weeks.

00:33:45.369 --> 00:33:47.445
I'll take that,
I'll take the big long term bets.

00:33:47.445 --> 00:33:50.816
Don't come to me with in between.

00:33:50.816 --> 00:33:54.961
&gt;&gt; One thing when you were describing kind
of the product sweep that you're most

00:33:54.961 --> 00:33:59.184
focused about that I noticed is that you
did not mention autonomous vehicles.

00:33:59.184 --> 00:34:02.597
&gt;&gt; That's part of the ride-share business.

00:34:02.597 --> 00:34:06.820
With autonomous, what's different
about us, as it relates to

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:11.525
other companies out there is Autonomous
is autonomy, means to an end.

00:34:11.525 --> 00:34:16.393
Now, autonomous technology is going to be
incredibly exciting in terms of safety and

00:34:16.393 --> 00:34:20.286
ultimately cost and
providing the services, transportation and

00:34:20.286 --> 00:34:22.859
mobility service to everyone everywhere,

00:34:22.859 --> 00:34:26.143
especially if you don't live
in the middle of the city.

00:34:26.143 --> 00:34:31.036
But I don't believe that autonomous could
be commercialized at the giant scale

00:34:31.036 --> 00:34:34.332
that it has a potential to do so
without ride sharing.

00:34:34.332 --> 00:34:38.166
And I believe that any rideshare service
that doesn't have autonomous 15 to 20

00:34:38.166 --> 00:34:39.436
years from now will exist.

00:34:39.436 --> 00:34:44.540
So to us, autonomous and rideshare
ultimately are going to be one and

00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:47.539
the same and the advantage that we have.

00:34:47.539 --> 00:34:51.453
The magic is that we are able
to have them all under one roof.

00:34:51.453 --> 00:34:57.452
When we develop autonomous, we can
develop it for a very small use case.

00:34:57.452 --> 00:34:59.815
Which is let's say for
one percent, by the way,

00:34:59.815 --> 00:35:03.794
that very small use case is going to be
very difficult, but one percent of routes,

00:35:03.794 --> 00:35:06.697
then two percent of routes,
then three percent of routes.

00:35:06.697 --> 00:35:11.753
The challenges that we have to take
on in commercialization are much,

00:35:11.753 --> 00:35:17.076
much more simple than the challenges
that any autonomous-only player.

00:35:17.076 --> 00:35:20.965
So for us, autonomous and ride share,
they're ultimately going to be one and

00:35:20.965 --> 00:35:21.833
the same for us.

00:35:21.833 --> 00:35:25.638
&gt;&gt; Do you think, on the supply side,
eventually Uber might serve as a platform

00:35:25.638 --> 00:35:29.995
for players, right now, who are saying
they're autonomous only, like Waymo?

00:35:29.995 --> 00:35:30.732
&gt;&gt; Very much so.

00:35:30.732 --> 00:35:35.140
Yeah, we will welcome all comers,
and you'll see that now.

00:35:35.140 --> 00:35:40.486
For example,
we have an integration with Lime.

00:35:40.486 --> 00:35:45.268
They have bikes and scooters out there,
already I think they are live in Oakland.

00:35:45.268 --> 00:35:49.850
So ultimately the vision that we see
of Uber is, we want to be that A to B

00:35:49.850 --> 00:35:54.676
mobility and transportation platform for
people, food, and things.

00:35:54.676 --> 00:35:59.327
And no one company can provide
all of the different versions of

00:35:59.327 --> 00:36:01.483
transportation mobility.

00:36:01.483 --> 00:36:05.685
We will look to integrate in all
other many many other third parties

00:36:05.685 --> 00:36:10.414
just like Amazon has their own product and
third party merchant product and

00:36:10.414 --> 00:36:13.942
we want part of that mobility
to be hopefully provided by

00:36:13.942 --> 00:36:16.973
cities the bus service,
Metro service, etc.

00:36:16.973 --> 00:36:20.798
We want to have all of that available
in Uber so that if you say,

00:36:20.798 --> 00:36:25.381
I want to get to the city now, what is
the best way for you to get to the city?

00:36:25.381 --> 00:36:30.464
And if the best way for you is to take
Caltrain, we will tell you exactly when

00:36:30.464 --> 00:36:35.551
it's coming, we'll make payment perfect
and we will make it predictable

00:36:35.551 --> 00:36:40.475
as far as you're having real time data and
hopefully at some point we will

00:36:40.475 --> 00:36:44.948
introduce surged pricing in all
the other cool stuff that I we do.

00:36:44.948 --> 00:36:45.919
&gt;&gt; A lot to do.

00:36:45.919 --> 00:36:46.512
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:46.512 --> 00:36:50.977
&gt;&gt; And I want to turn to you now,
I might understand that you even

00:36:50.977 --> 00:36:55.959
commuting to the Bay area from Seattle
&gt;&gt; The commute has officially stopped.

00:36:55.959 --> 00:36:56.522
&gt;&gt; Congratulations.

00:36:56.522 --> 00:36:57.460
&gt;&gt; Yes, I have moved.

00:36:57.460 --> 00:36:58.319
Thank you very much.

00:36:58.319 --> 00:37:00.301
&gt;&gt; That's fabulous news.

00:37:00.301 --> 00:37:01.973
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:37:01.973 --> 00:37:06.368
&gt;&gt; Internally at Uber, you are riding the
ship, while simultaneously preparing for

00:37:06.368 --> 00:37:06.944
this IPO.

00:37:06.944 --> 00:37:09.752
You're also managing
the pressure of internal leaks,

00:37:09.752 --> 00:37:12.811
an all hands meeting was recently
leaked just a day or so ago.

00:37:12.811 --> 00:37:17.402
&gt;&gt; Yes,
the leads has stopped until yesterday, so.

00:37:17.402 --> 00:37:19.757
But you are working to
repair trust with an Uber.

00:37:19.757 --> 00:37:20.803
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:37:20.803 --> 00:37:23.478
&gt;&gt; How that's a lot of tension.

00:37:23.478 --> 00:37:24.018
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:24.018 --> 00:37:26.117
&gt;&gt; How do you maintain your sanity?

00:37:26.117 --> 00:37:27.200
Who's your support system.

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:31.378
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I'd say my wife.

00:37:31.378 --> 00:37:36.507
You know she's a I'd
say there two elements.

00:37:36.507 --> 00:37:43.664
One is When you come from a family whose
lost everything and then rebuilt it.

00:37:43.664 --> 00:37:48.591
It puts a perspective which
is I just kind of don't have

00:37:48.591 --> 00:37:53.237
a fear of loss anymore because
I know I'm going to be ok.

00:37:53.237 --> 00:37:57.549
So the leak did it suck yeah,
but are things getting better?

00:37:57.549 --> 00:37:59.412
Much better.

00:37:59.412 --> 00:38:03.077
And then when I get home and
you know I have dinner with my wife and

00:38:03.077 --> 00:38:06.966
my kids it's all good, and
the next day I'll do my best.

00:38:06.966 --> 00:38:13.687
And you know what I know I always
have with me is my family.

00:38:13.687 --> 00:38:19.395
And what I know I will be true to
is really giving everything I have

00:38:19.395 --> 00:38:24.680
to this opportunity because it
is an opportunity of a lifetime,

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:28.718
and I'm incredibly lucky
to be in this spot.

00:38:28.718 --> 00:38:32.822
&gt;&gt; It's interesting because before
you came to Uber and Expedia,

00:38:32.822 --> 00:38:34.726
you tried to travel to India.

00:38:34.726 --> 00:38:36.071
Your Visa got denied.

00:38:36.071 --> 00:38:37.014
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:38:37.014 --> 00:38:40.081
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But-
&gt;&gt; Apparently I wasn't a big deal enough.

00:38:40.081 --> 00:38:41.850
Uber, I got a visa like that.

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:42.862
It was amazing.

00:38:42.862 --> 00:38:45.310
&gt;&gt; And not only that, but
you were meeting with Prime Minister Modi.

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:45.952
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:38:45.952 --> 00:38:52.010
&gt;&gt; And equally as CEO, you are making
